      "vocab_aquifer": "An underground layer of water-bearing permeable rock, gravel, sand, or silt from which groundwater can be extracted.",
      "vocab_bowen_ratio": "The ratio of sensible heat to latent heat loss from the surface, determining local climate feedbacks.",
      "vocab_clausius_clapeyron": "The thermodynamic relationship showing that atmospheric moisture capacity increases by about 7% per degree Celsius of warming.",
      "vocab_collection": "The accumulation of water in oceans, lakes, rivers, and underground reservoirs, completing the surface loop.",
      "vocab_condensation": "The process where water vapor gas cools and changes back into liquid water droplets, forming clouds.",
      "vocab_darcy_s_law": "A mathematical equation that describes the flow of a fluid through a porous medium, governing groundwater.",
      "vocab_evaporation": "The process where liquid water absorbs thermal energy and changes into water vapor gas, rising into the atmosphere.",
      "vocab_infiltration": "The process by which water on the ground surface enters soil and rock; some infiltrated water can contribute to groundwater recharge.",
      "vocab_latent_heat": "The heat energy absorbed or released by water during a phase change (like liquid to gas) without changing temperature.",
      "vocab_pfas": "Synthetic per- and polyfluoroalkyl substances that contaminate water supplies and resist natural breakdown.",
      "vocab_precipitation": "Water falling from clouds to Earth's surface as rain, snow, sleet, or hail when droplets grow too heavy.",
      "vocab_riparian_buffer": "A vegetated area next to a water body, usually forested, which helps shade streams and filter run-off.",
      "vocab_sublimation": "The direct transition of water from solid ice or snow into water vapor gas, bypassing the liquid phase.",
      "vocab_transpiration": "The evaporation of water from plant leaves through tiny pores called stomata, acting as a natural pump.",
      "vocab_watershed": "An area of land where all of the water that falls in it drains off into a common outlet like a river or bay.",
