      "seismic_engineering_for_bridges": "🌎 Seismic engineering for bridges",
      "seismic_retrofit_success_the_californi": "Seismic retrofit success — the California program: ",
      "self_propelled_modular_transporters_sp": "Self-propelled modular transporters (SPMTs)",
      "several_states_have_developed_pre_engi": "Several states have developed pre-engineered standard bridges for the most common short-span applications. The Vermont Agency of Transportation's ABC standard plans cover spans from 25-80 feet, with detailed precast deck + abutment specs that any local contractor can build. The Federal Lands Highway Program has similar plans for remote sites. The advantage: instead of paying for a custom design ($100K+ engineering), a small-town bridge replacement can use a standard plan ($5-10K design fee) + standard precast pieces. Lead time drops from 18 months to 6 months for a typical rural bridge.",
      "shallower_sag_less_drop_in_the_cable_m": "Shallower sag (less drop in the cable) means the cable is more horizontal, which means MORE horizontal tension is needed to support the same deck weight. Cable tension scales as 1/sag — halving the sag doubles the cable force. Real suspension bridges use sag/span ratios around 1:8 to 1:12 to balance cable cost (more sag = lower force = thinner cable) against tower height (more sag = taller towers needed to clear the deck above water/road). Golden Gate sag ratio: 1:11. Akashi-Kaikyō: 1:10.",
      "shear_stress": "Shear stress τ",
      "show_reference_tabs": "Show reference tabs",
      "silver_bridge_1967_fatigue_corrosion": "Silver Bridge 1967 — fatigue + corrosion: ",
      "sketch_3_different_bridge_types_you_co": "Sketch 3 different bridge types you could use. Don't commit yet.",
      "slope_grade": "Slope + grade",
      "soft_clay_silt": "Soft clay / silt",
      "solid_rock_bedrock": "Solid rock (bedrock)",
      "some_iconic_bridges_win_awards_for_the": "Some \"iconic\" bridges win awards for the architect and burden the community with maintenance costs the original budget did not contain (several Calatrava bridges have faced lawsuits over this). The reverse is also true: many beloved century-old bridges were not considered beautiful when new (the Eiffel Tower was originally hated by Parisian artists). Time, use, and care are part of what makes a bridge become iconic — not just the original sketch.",
      "some_materials_have_very_high_strength": "Some materials have very high strength but are heavy or expensive in real life. The simulation does not penalize this. What would change about your hypotheses if cost mattered?",
      "some_steels_have_a_special_property_be": "Some steels have a special property — below a stress threshold (~100 MPa for typical structural steel), fatigue cracks essentially don't grow. This \"endurance limit\" lets steel bridges last centuries IF designed conservatively below it. Aluminum and most non-ferrous metals have NO endurance limit — they always fatigue eventually.",
      "sources_ngss_lead_states_2013_hibbeler": "Sources: NGSS Lead States (2013) · Hibbeler, R.C. (2017), Structural Analysis · AASHTO LRFD Bridge Design Specifications · Petroski, H. (1994), Design Paradigms (Tacoma Narrows + Tay Bridge + Hyatt). Printed from AlloFlow STEAM Lab.",
      "span_length": "Span length: ",
      "span_length_in_meters": "Span length in meters",
      "span_m": "Span (m)",
      "span_m_2": "Span (m)",
      "span_m_3": "Span (m)",
      "spec": "Spec",
      "specify_minimum_recycled_steel_content": "Specify minimum recycled steel content (typically 70-90% achievable). Replace 30-50% of cement with fly ash, slag, or calcined clay.",
      "spmts_are_massive_multi_axle_hydraulic": "SPMTs are massive multi-axle hydraulically-leveled platforms that can carry entire bridge spans — sometimes thousands of tons — and roll them into place along a highway closure. Each axle has its own steering + hydraulic suspension, so the SPMT can crab sideways, rotate in place, and self-level over uneven ground. Multiple SPMT trailers can be linked together. The Massena Bridge replacement (Massachusetts, 2014) used SPMTs to roll a 416-ton bridge span ~5 miles from a staging area + lower it onto its piers in a single 55-hour weekend closure. Total bridge replacement: 4 days. Conventional construction would have taken 6+ months of partial closure.",
      "spread_caisson": "Spread caisson",
      "spread_footing": "Spread footing",
      "standard_abc_bridge_designs": "Standard ABC bridge designs",
      "status_cost": "Material cost",
      "status_material": "Material",
      "status_stress": "Stress",
      "stays_in_tension": "Stays in tension",
      "stays_per_side": "Stays per side",
      "steel_concrete_jacketing": "Steel + concrete jacketing",
      "steel_desc": "Strong in BOTH tension and compression. Ductile (bends before breaking, giving warning). The workhorse of modern bridge engineering.",
      "stiff_clay": "Stiff clay",
      "still_used": "Still used",
      "stone_desc": "Very strong in compression, weak in tension. Heavy. Lasts millennia if well-built. Roman arch bridges from 100 BCE still stand.",
      "strain_gauges": "Strain gauges",
      "strain_gauges_accelerometers": "Strain gauges + accelerometers",
      "strength": "Strength",
      "stress_amplitude": "Stress amplitude",
      "stress_amplitude_mpa": "Stress amplitude (MPa)",
      "stress_analysis_deep_beam_approximatio": "Stress analysis (deep-beam approximation)",
      "stress_test": "Stress Test",
      "students_can_build_a_simple_section_mo": "Students can build a simple section-model wind tunnel from a box fan, a length of cardboard for an airfoil-like deck cross-section, and rubber bands as springs. Hang the cross-section on rubber bands in the airflow + slowly increase the fan speed. Bluff-shaped sections (rectangular cardboard) flutter at relatively low wind speeds; streamlined sections (curved + tapered) remain stable at higher speeds. This is exactly the same principle that distinguished the original Tacoma deck from modern long-span bridge cross-sections. The Khan Academy + Bridge Design + Engineering YouTube channels have demonstrations.",
      "submersible_submerged_floating_bridges": "Submersible (submerged-floating) bridges",
      "subsea_bored_tunnel": "Subsea bored tunnel",
      "success_cases": "Success cases",
      "sundial_bridge": "Sundial Bridge",
      "supports_pin_left_roller_right": "Supports: pin (left) ▲  ·  roller (right) ▲⊙",
      "surface_materials": "Surface materials",
      "suspension_bridge_cable_shape": "Suspension bridge cable shape",
      "suspension_bridges_work_differently_fr": "Suspension bridges work differently from trusses. The deck hangs from cables; cables hang in a curve between towers; towers transfer the load down. The cables are in PURE TENSION, the towers in PURE COMPRESSION. Anchorages resist the horizontal pull of the cables — they're typically massive concrete blocks buried in bedrock at each end of the bridge.",
      "suspension_desc": "The deck hangs from cables, which hang in a curve between two tall towers. The cables are in tension; the towers are in compression. Anchorages at each end resist the cable tension.",
      "sustainable_design_strategies": "Sustainable design strategies: ",
      "sustained_high_wind_sites": "Sustained high-wind sites",
      "swing_bridge": "Swing bridge",
      "synchronization_beginning": "Synchronization beginning: ",
