      "wasd_fly_e_sonar_p_perch_r_restart": "WASD=fly • E=sonar • P=perch • R=restart",
      "water_molecules_are_closer_together_tr": "Water molecules are closer together, transmitting vibrations much faster. This is why dolphin sonar works at much greater distances than bat sonar.",
      "wave_speed": "Wave Speed",
      "wave_visualizer": "Wave Visualizer",
      "wavelength": "Wavelength",
      "wavelength_2": "Wavelength",
      "wavelength_determines_resolution_a_50_": "Wavelength determines resolution. A 50 kHz bat call in air has λ = 6.9 mm — perfect for detecting insects.",
      "webgl_is_required_to_render_the_3d_cav": "WebGL is required to render the 3D cave environment. Please check your browser settings or hardware acceleration and try again.",
      "what_you_can_do": "What YOU Can Do",
      "what_you_re_learning": "🧠 What you're learning",
      "what_you_re_seeing": "What you're seeing:",
      "when_a_sound_source_and_a_listener_mov": "When a sound source and a listener move relative to each other, the listener hears a different frequency than what was emitted. Same physics that makes a passing ambulance siren drop in pitch as it goes by, and the same trick a bat uses to read whether prey is approaching or fleeing.",
      "when_the_pulse_hits_an_object_some_ene": "When the pulse hits an object, some energy bounces back as an echo. Hard surfaces (rock, chitin) reflect strongly. Soft surfaces (fur, carpet) absorb most energy. The object's shape affects the echo pattern.",
      "where_n_number_of_chirps_in_14_seconds": "where N₁₄ = number of chirps in 14 seconds",
      "while_insectivorous_bats_are_aerial_hu": "While insectivorous bats are aerial hunters, fruit bats use a completely different foraging strategy. In the simulator's frugivore mode, you experience how Egyptian fruit bats navigate:",
      "white_nose_syndrome": "White-Nose Syndrome",
      "white_nose_syndrome_wns_is_caused_by_t": "White-nose syndrome (WNS) is caused by the fungus Pseudogymnoascus destructans. It grows on the skin of hibernating bats, irritating them and causing them to wake up repeatedly during winter. Each arousal burns precious fat reserves. By spring, infected bats have starved to death.",
      "why_bats_matter_ecosystem_services": "Why Bats Matter — Ecosystem Services",
      "wind_turbine_barotrauma": "Wind Turbine Barotrauma",
      "wing_loading_10_200_n_m_varies_by_spec": "• Wing loading: 10-200 N/m² (varies by species)",
      "wing_loading_5_15_n_m_very_light": "• Wing loading: 5-15 N/m² (very light)",
      "with_over_1_400_species_bats_have_evol": "With over 1,400 species, bats have evolved an incredible range of diets. The two playable species in the simulator represent the two largest dietary groups, but bats eat much more:",
      "you_can_change_species_anytime_using_t": "You can change species anytime using the toggle in the game HUD.",
      "bat_desc": "Bat calls are ultrasonic — above human hearing. Shown here scaled down. In reality you would hear nothing!",
      "cricket_desc": "Male crickets chirp by rubbing their wings together (stridulation). Count chirps in 14 seconds and add 40 to estimate temperature in °F!",
      "dolphin_desc": "Dolphins evolved echolocation independently from bats — convergent evolution! They use clicks focused through the melon (forehead) and receive echoes through their jaw.",
      "echolocation_visualization": "Echolocation visualization",
      "echolocation_visualization_2": "Echolocation visualization",
      "echolocation_visualization_3": "Echolocation visualization",
      "echolocation_visualization_4": "Echolocation visualization",
      "echolocation_visualization_5": "Echolocation visualization",
      "echolocation_visualization_6": "Echolocation visualization",
      "echolocation_visualization_7": "Echolocation visualization",
      "frog_desc": "Male frogs call to attract mates. Each species has a unique call frequency so females find the right species even in a noisy chorus.",
      "frugivore_desc": "A larger fruit bat that uses tongue-click echolocation and excellent night vision. Your sonar is weaker but you can see ripe fruit glowing faintly.",
      "frugivore_subtitle": "Fruit Forager",
      "insectivore_desc": "A tiny insectivorous bat that uses rapid-fire echolocation to hunt moths and mosquitoes in the dark. Your sonar is powerful and precise.",
      "insectivore_subtitle": "Insect Hunter",
      "owl_desc": "Owls hoot at low frequencies that travel far through forests. Their asymmetric ears let them pinpoint prey by sound alone in total darkness.",
      "whale_desc": "Whale songs can travel thousands of miles underwater. At 188 decibels, blue whale calls are the loudest sound made by any animal."
